---
// Lesson 3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning & Playtest
// Close the turret arc: difficulty, tuning, and the partner playtest.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.4a — Turret Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The turret works. Working is not the same as fun. Today you turn
    "it shoots" into "I want to try again" — with the tuning process
    designers actually use, and the playtest that proves it.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>The turret's difficulty lives in exactly four numbers, and you
    own all of them now:</p>
    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob (lives on)</th><th>Default</th><th>Turns into</th></tr>
        <tr><td>maxAttackRange (turret)</td><td>7</td><td>How much of the room is dangerous</td></tr>
        <tr><td>attackCooldown (turret)</td><td>2s</td><td>How much time between chances to dodge</td></tr>
        <tr><td>speed (projectile prefab)</td><td>10</td><td>How much reaction time you get</td></tr>
        <tr><td>damage (projectile prefab)</td><td>1</td><td>How much a mistake costs</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the tuning matrix.</strong> Try three turret
    personalities in one scene (three turrets, different knobs): a
    SNIPER (long range, slow shots, fast bullets), a SPRAYER (short
    range, fast shots), and a FLAK (medium range, slow big bullets,
    damage 2). Write the four numbers for each before you test — tune
    with a hypothesis, not a vibe.</p>

    <p><strong>Step 2 — difficulty curves.</strong> Place the three so
    the player meets SPRAYER first, FLAK second, SNIPER last. That
    ramp — easy threat, medium threat, precision threat — is a
    difficulty curve built from copies of one prefab. You will reuse
    this exact idea at scale in <a href="7.5a.html">7.5a</a>, when the
    whole cave is a curve.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The unfair-feeling fight</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Build a turret the player CANNOT dodge: range 12,
                cooldown 0.2, speed 20. Play it. What is the difference
                between HARD and IMPOSSIBLE, in one sentence each?</li>
            <li>Now make one that LOOKS scary but is dodgeable
                (range 10, cooldown 1, speed 6). Which numbers make
                fear — and which make failure?</li>
            <li>Set projectile damage to 3 with default everything else.
                What does one hit do to the whole game's health
                economy (remember: max 3)?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Swap seats (<a href="2.4a.html">2.4a</a> rules: tester plays and
        talks, designer watches silently). Watch for the three tells:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Never approached</strong> — the radius reads as a
            wall. Shrink range or telegraph it.</li>
        <li><strong>Tanked on purpose</strong> — bullets cost less than
            the detour. Raise damage or place coins on the safe path.</li>
        <li><strong>Died the same way twice</strong> — the failure is
            unclear. Slower bullets, louder fire moment.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Close the loop</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your top-3 fixes from your partner's run, then
                apply exactly one. Re-run the partner. Did the tell
                change? One variable at a time is how you learn WHICH
                change mattered.</li>
            <li>Where in YOUR overworld should the first turret go —
                before or after the potion? Defend it in two
                sentences using health economy, not taste.</li>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"3.4a: turret personalities + tuning matrix"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Name the four difficulty knobs and what each controls in play</li>
            <li>Design three distinct turret personalities from one prefab</li>
            <li>Explain hard vs impossible vs scary, with numbers</li>
            <li>Run a silent playtest, pick the top fix, apply exactly one</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/index.html">Unit 3: The Turret</a> — the complete annotated turret</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the five patterns</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="4.1a.html">4.1a — The Enemy: Waypoint Patrols</a> — Unit 4 begins.</p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.4a" title="3.4a Turret Damage, Tuning &amp; Playtest — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
